WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.960
In this problem, a big fancy house is built as shown and in parts 300 pounds per square foot

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:13.160
of vertical stress onto the ground beneath it.

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:18.740
Find the effective vertical stress in pounds per square foot at the given point in the

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:22.879
soil profile depicted.

00:00:22.879 --> 00:00:29.280
So in the given figure, we can see our big fancy house imparting 300 PSF to the soil beneath

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:37.640
that we can see point A located in the clay and we can see all of the respective depths

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:40.640
relative to each other item.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:46.200
We also have the groundwater level called out and the respective densities of the sand and

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:48.680
the clay.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:54.960
So all we need to do is find the effective vertical stress at point A. So the first thing

00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:03.159
we can do is note that the stress increases both with the depth and the density or unit

00:01:03.159 --> 00:01:07.560
weight of whatever soil is above that depth.

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:13.599
So the first thing we can do is consider the stress just from the house and this number

00:01:13.599 --> 00:01:18.359
is given to us directly as 300 PSF.

00:01:18.359 --> 00:01:22.760
Keep in mind that those are the units we are looking for anyway.

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:27.640
Next we need to find the effective stress in just the dry sand.

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:32.960
So the section of sand to the depth of the groundwater level will be dry and that's going

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:35.880
to be 8 feet.

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:40.719
So the effective vertical stress for this layer is going to be the unit weight of the dry

00:01:40.719 --> 00:01:44.600
sand times the depth of the dry sand.

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:48.760
And this is going to give us 880 pounds per square foot.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:02:01.040
Next we can calculate the effective vertical stress in the saturated sand.

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:06.280
So if the total depth of the sand is 10 feet and the total dry depth is 8 feet then the

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:11.920
total saturated sand is going to be 2 feet.

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:16.759
So when calculating the saturated effective vertical stress it's important to remember that

00:02:16.759 --> 00:02:23.639
any load carried by the saturated soil is carried by both the grains of soil and also

00:02:23.639 --> 00:02:27.079
the poor pressure of the water.

00:02:27.079 --> 00:02:32.079
So in this case we are actually going to be subtracting out the density of water from the

00:02:32.079 --> 00:02:35.479
density of our saturated sand.

00:02:35.479 --> 00:02:43.959
So first we are going to subtract 110 pounds per cubic foot by 62.4 pounds per cubic foot

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:46.840
which is the unit weight of water.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:52.040
And you can look that up in the reference manual if you don't have that memorized.

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:56.360
And we are going to multiply it by the saturated depth of 2 feet.

00:02:56.360 --> 00:03:01.800
And this is going to give us about 95.2 pounds per foot squared.

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:07.360
And finally we can solve for the effective vertical stress in the clay up to the given point

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:08.719
here.

00:03:08.719 --> 00:03:13.719
So if the total depth of clay is 10 feet and we know the point is 5 feet above the

00:03:13.719 --> 00:03:19.199
bottom of the clay layer then the depth of clay remaining will be 5 feet.

00:03:19.199 --> 00:03:26.879
So just as we did before with the saturated sand we can subtract 62.4 from the unit weight

00:03:26.879 --> 00:03:39.000
of the clay.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:42.800
And then multiply by the depth to which we are going.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:47.480
And this will give us 188 pounds per square foot.

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:54.360
So if we sum up each of the vertical stresses that we calculated from 1 to 4 we are going

00:03:54.360 --> 00:04:01.560
to see that our total effect of vertical stress is about 1463 pounds per square foot which

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:05.680
we can see is our second answer.

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:10.640
A few things to point out for this problem would be to first remember to account for the

00:04:10.679 --> 00:04:16.479
ground water at the depth that it exists and not to try to solve the problem for a deeper

00:04:16.479 --> 00:04:21.079
point in the layer than the point being asked for.

00:04:21.079 --> 00:04:28.719
Finally remember to account for the stress in the house that was given directly.

00:04:28.719 --> 00:04:35.399
The height of the house does not matter but the height of the soil layers does because

00:04:35.399 --> 00:04:40.159
we were only given density instead of effective stress.

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:41.840
But that is all it takes to solve this problem.
